What Limits Growth in Frame Unit Sales?
Reusable frames are an important constraint on replacement volume. A clean, dimensionally stable stainless-steel ring may support repeated wafer mounting and processing before retirement. New purchases can therefore reflect line expansion or wear rather than every incremental wafer run. Suppliers cannot assume volume grows at the same rate as wafer starts.

Polymer frames face a different set of trade-offs. One-way logistics can avoid return transport and cleaning steps, but generate additional material use and disposal considerations. Plastic stiffness, surface finish and electrostatic behavior can also require careful validation. Tool makers and packaging contractors may restrict accepted frame outlines, creating both a qualification barrier and a need for precise catalog management.

